This document contains the basics for running and using the
PEBIL binary instrumentation package with EP Analytics characterization
tools.. Direct any questions or comments to allysonc@epanalytics.com

SST users, please use the README_FOR_SST.md file instead.

1. Installation - Please see /path/to/pebil/INSTALL

2. Source PEBIL - Bring the PEBIL into your environment:

      cd /path/to/pebil
      source bashrc

NOTE: To get line information the application must be compiled with -g,
      recompile before instrumenting application. This is not necessary for
      SST.

3. Instrument the application:

      pebil --tool <ChosenTool> --app <YourApplication> --saveall

   See below for detailed tool descriptions but suggested initial
   instrumentation tools:
        pebil --tool FunctionCoverage --app <APP> --saveall
        pebil --tool BasicBlockCounter --app <APP> --saveall
        pebil --tool FunctionTimer --app <APP> --lnc libtimer.so --saveall
        pebil --tool ArielIntercept --app <APP> --inp + --saveall
       

   Other options to consider:

     --threaded    required for OpenMP applications

     --sanitize    option to sanitize function names for sensitive apps

     --fbl <file>  option if you would like to exclude or 
                   include certain functions 
                   (See below details on how and when to use)

4. Set tool parameters through the environment.
   For example, for function timer:

        export FTIMER_SHUTOFF=1
        export FTIMER_CPU_FREQ=200000000 <---set this for CPU freq in Hz

   More information about these environment variables 
   is available below.

5. Run the instrumented binary as you would the application. 
   PEBIL will produce an instrumented binary which is the name of the
   application followed by a special suffix (e.g.  <YourApplication>.jbbinst). 

   For example, if you normally run your application like:

        mpirun -np 32 ./theApp -x 4 -y 4 -z 2 < input.txt

   You would run the instrumented application like: 

        mpirun -np 32 ./theApp.jbbinst -x 4 -y 4 -z 2 < input.txt
        NOTE that pebil environment set by step 2 must be included in any
        run/batch script.

*********************************************************************
Exclude list or  include list functions: 
EXCLUDE LIST:
Often in performing detailed characterization of large binaries there will
be functions that cause instrumentation issues, this is common for certain
Intel compiler-inserted functions, for example. 
To avoid these issues you can add these to an exclude list
where they will not be instrumented.
         For an exclude list, create a file with the functions
         you don't want instrumented, one on each line:

             function1
             function2
             function3
             ...

INCLUDE LIST:
Similar to the exclude list you can specify only those functions you want to
instrument. This can be helpful to reduce the size of the instrumented binary
for only user defined functions or only user defined functions that are
utilized for a given input. 
        For an include list, create a file with the functions
        you want instrumented, one on each line, and add
        a * at the top of the file:

             *
             function1
             function2
             function3

*********************************************************************

EP Analytics Characterization Tools for PEBIL

Each tool creates an instrumented binary and *hiddenFunctions file. The 
*hiddenFunctions file contains a list of functions that we not able to be
instrumented with the current flags. 

1. Function Coverage
   A simple tool that tracks function coverage.
   Useful to narrow down the focus of performance analysis to just
   the functions that are executed, this can reduce the instrumented binary size.

      pebil --tool FunctionCoverage --app <APP> ...

   Generates an instrumented binary called <APP>.fcinst
   Running the instrumented binary will produce one output file per
   MPI rank. The files are named <APP>.r<RANK_NUM>.t<TOTAL_RANKS>.fcinst.
   RANK_NUM is represented as an eight digit decimal number with 0's 
   padded; same is true for the TOTAL_RANKS.

   If one desires to create an include list of ONLY those functions that were
   executed:

   cat <APP>.r0*fcinst | grep ^BLK | awk '{print $5}' | sort -u > inclist.txt

   DO NOT FORGET to add a "*" at the top of the "inclist.txt" file. 

2. Basic Block Counting
   A low-impact tool that reports how often code was executed. 
   Useful for determining where to start performance analysis.

       pebil --tool BasicBlockCounter --app <APP> ...

   Generates an instrumented binary called <APP>.jbbinst

3. Function Timing
   A tool for timing how long the application spends in each 
   function. Useful for performing a hot-spot analysis

      pebil --tool FunctionTimer --app <APP> --lnc libtimer.so ...

   Generates an instrumented binary called <APP>.ftiminst

   Consider setting the following environment variables:

      A. FTIMER_CPU_FREQ=<FREQ>
         Set the CPU frequency (in Hz).

      B. FTIMER_SHUTOFF=1
         Turn off small functions which may report skewed times 
         due to the overhead of timing the function
   

